    "summary": "Epic Dark Star > Stella Blue > Eyes stretch with soaring Sunshine",
    "blurb": "The second‑set centerpiece—a 28‑minute Dark Star that flows into a luminous Stella Blue and then into Eyes of the World—defines this show. The first set opens bright with Here Comes Sunshine and tight, energetic renditions of Jack Straw and Playing In The Band. Soundboard recordings capture the band in top form, making it a must‑listen for any 1973 era collector.",
    "review": "October 30, 1973 at St. Louis’ Kiel Auditorium finds the Dead firing on all cylinders. A bright Here Comes Sunshine kicks off the evening, leading into a tight first set that builds momentum through Jack Straw and an inspired China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue. The real fireworks erupt in the second set: Dark Star unfurls for almost half an hour, its soaring guitar lines and Phil’s booming bass giving way to a transcendent Stella Blue before collapsing into an exploratory Eyes of the World. The band then rides the Weather Report Suite into a jubilant Let It Grow, closes with a rollicking Goin’ Down The Road Feeling Bad → Johnny B. Goode, and caps the night with One More Saturday Night. Keith’s jazzy organ textures add depth to the jams, while Bill’s steady drumming keeps everything grounded. Across multiple soundboard sources the performance shines—especially on the Hamilton tape, which offers crystal‑clear separation and no dropouts—making this a standout entry in the 1973 catalog.",
